        "video-consent": \{\
            "kind": "exp-lookit-video-consent",\
            "template": "consent_005",\
            "PIName": "Jenny Wang",\
            "institution": "Rutgers University - New Brunswick",\
            "PIContact": "child.research@psych.rutgers.edu",\
            "purpose": "This study is part of a larger research program aiming to understand how infants and children develop their understanding of objects and quantities.",\
            "procedures": "We conduct studies with infants and children of all ages. For infant studies, infants are shown videos and images on the screen and we measure what they are interested in by recording their eye-movements. You may be asked to close your eyes while your child watches videos so that your behavior doesn\'92t influence where your child looks. Children may be asked to make judgments, answer questions (such as making predictions and providing explanations), learn from and teach others, observe events, and perform actions such pointing to parts of the screen. You and your child may be asked to answer a brief set of questions. There are no \'93right or wrong\'94 responses to any of these questions or tasks, we simply want to find out how children of different ages understand and learn about the world.",\
            "voluntary_participation": "",\
            "risk_statement": "The risks associated with participation in this study are no greater than those encountered in daily life. ",\
            "payment": "Parents often feel that they benefit from this experience because learning more about child development is interesting and enjoyable. Children often find our studies fun! This study may benefit society if the results lead to a better understanding of children\'92s behavior.",\
            "include_databrary": true,\
            "gdpr": false,\
            "research_rights_statement": "You are not waiving any legal claims, rights or remedies because of your participation in this research study. If you feel you have been treated unfairly, or you have questions regarding your rights as a research subject, you may contact the Rutgers IRB Director at: Arts and Sciences IRB, 335 George St., Liberty Plaza Ste. 3200, New Brunswick, NJ 08901 (732) 235-2866 or the Rutgers Human Subjects Protection Program at (973) 972-1149, email us at humansubjects@ored.rutgers.edu."\
        \},\
        "exit-survey": \{\
            "kind": "exp-lookit-exit-survey",\
            "debriefing": \{\
                "title": "Thank you for participating!",\
                "blocks": [\
                    \{\
                        "title": "",\
                        "text": "This research wouldn't be possible without awesome parents like you."\
                    \},\
                    \{\
                        "title": "Some Background Information:",\
                        "text": "We make inferences about others all the time, ranging from what direction the person walking in front of us will go in, to thinking about what our friend might like to receive as a gift. <a href=https://kids.frontiersin.org/articles/10.3389/frym.2019.00101 target=\'91_blank\'92>This article</a> nicely summarized how come we are so good at figuring out what others think. \\n\\n We are particularly curious to see what people think about others\'92 abilities - such as another child, or a monkey, or an ant.\\n\\n Previously, we found that adults make inferences about others\'92 numerical abilities, and these beliefs vary depending on who they\'92re thinking about.\\n\\n In the current study we ask whether children have similar intuitions about other people and creatures' abilities as adults, and if not, how our intuitions change as we get older. We study this by showing children pictures of choice scenarios and ask children to tell us what they think.\\n\\n This study will help us better understand how children reason about the mind of others, and how might their intuition develop over time."\
                    \},\
                    \{\
                        "title": "Compensation:",\
                        "text": "This research would not be possible without awesome parents like you. Thank you again for your participation! We will provide a $5 USD Amazon gift card for eligible participants. We will send you 1 gift card within 1 week of your first eligible participation. To be eligible for compensation we ask that you 1) provide a valid consent video (we will show you how!), 2) your child must be in the age range specified above and, 3) your child must be present during the recorded videos. Your child does not need to finish the entire study in order to be eligible for compensation. \\n\\nPlease tell your friends about your experience. We are always looking for new little scientists to join our community!"\
                    \},\
                    \{\
                        "title": "Questions or Concerns:",\
                        "text": "Please do not hesitate to contact Rahma Mbarki at child.research@psych.rutgers.edu."\
                    \}\
                ]\
            \}\
        \},\
